{"uid":"progs/doomgeneric/doomgeneric_minios.c::MINIOS_DOOM_BACKBUF_ADDR","name":"MINIOS_DOOM_BACKBUF_ADDR","qualname":"MINIOS_DOOM_BACKBUF_ADDR","kind":"function","file":"progs/doomgeneric/doomgeneric_minios.c","line":4,"end_line":4,"language":"c","signature":"* MINIOS_DOOM_BACKBUF_ADDR (minios_abi.h);","params":[{"name":"h","annotation":"minios_abi.","default":"","kind":"positional"}],"returns":"*","decorators":[],"bases":[],"modifiers":[],"visibility":"public","doc":{"summary":"========================================================================= Graphics back-buffers (kernel-mapped into user window) ========================================================================= The kernel allocates these from the kernel heap and maps them into the user window via the 4 KB page tables at boot (mm_setup_protections).","description":"A ring-3 program writes to these addresses and calls the corresponding SYS_*_FRAME syscall; the kernel composites the buffer onto the desktop.\n\nAll three sit in the reserved tail above DOOM_BACKBUF_ADDR (the brk cap),\nso a growing heap or mmap region can never reach them.  NK_BACKBUF sits\nHIGHER than the framebuffer's maximum span: at 1024x768x24/32 the\nframebuffer needs up to 3 MB, so a back-buffer at 0x0B400000 would overlap\nits tail and steal its bottom rows (the mapped back-buffer overwrote the\nframebuffer's second page-table slot, rendering the dock/taskbar black).\n=========================================================================","params":[],"returns":null,"yields":null,"raises":[],"attributes":[],"examples":[],"notes":[["Inherited","Documentation from `MINIOS_DOOM_BACKBUF_ADDR` (progs/minios_abi.h:140)."]],"see_also":[],"deprecated":"","since":"","style":"plain"},"parent":"progs/doomgeneric/doomgeneric_minios.c","members":[],"inferred_raises":[],"returns_value":null,"value":"","base_uids":[],"subclasses":[],"calls":[],"called_by":[]}
